---
import "./head.css";
---

<header class="page-head">
  <div>
    <div class="eyebrow">CSS tool</div>
    <h1 class="page-title">Corner-shape generator</h1>
  </div>
  <p class="page-intro">
    Drag the sliders until the corners look right, copy the CSS with fallback.
    Two tiers of fallback are available: the default one is a <code class="code"
      >border-radius</code
    > that removes the same amount of corner as the superellipse does, and the exact
    one traces the real curve with <code class="code">clip-path</code>.
  </p>
  <details class="about">
    <summary class="about-summary">In more detail →</summary>
    <div class="about-content">
      <p class="about-paragraph">
        The{" "}
        <a
          class="about-link"
          href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/corner-shape"
          target="_blank"><code class="code">corner-shape</code></a
        > property specifies the curve applied within the corner region defined by{
          " "
        }
        <code class="code">border-radius</code>. The value inside{" "}
        <code class="code">superellipse()</code> acts as an exponent (k = 2<sup
          >s</sup
        >): <strong>0</strong> produces a straight bevel, <strong>1</strong> gives
        you a regular circular curve, <strong>2</strong> matches Apple's iOS squircle,
        and negative values make the corner curve inward rather than outward. The
        scale runs out at both ends: below <strong>-3</strong> the spec's <em
          >-infinity</em
        > takes over as notch, above <strong>5</strong> its <em>infinity</em> takes
        over as square. Every step doubles <strong>k</strong>, and the curve
        flattens so fast toward straight lines that cranking the exponent higher
        barely moves it (we're talking less than a pixel of difference).
      </p>
      <p class="about-paragraph">
        If the newer syntax isn't supported, you can fall back in one of two
        ways. By default you fall back to good old <code class="code"
          >border-radius</code
        >, which plays nicely with borders, outlines, and shadows, but it can
        only round corners outward (no concave curves). The <strong
          >exact fallback</strong
        >{" "}
        uses <code class="code">clip-path</code> to trace the actual curve. It reproduces
        the shape exactly, but then cuts off anything rendered outside it, including
        your shadows and outlines.
      </p>
      <p class="about-paragraph">
        For outward-curving corners, the generator calculates a <code
          class="code">border-radius</code
        >{" "}
        that removes the same amount of corner area as the superellipse would, so
        both versions look visually equivalent. Concave curves can't be replicated
        with <code class="code">border-radius</code>, so the generator just
        keeps whatever radius you typed and warns you about it. When you're in
        exact mode with a custom curve, the generator pieces it together from
        Bézier segments, splitting them over and over until the fit is within
        0.25% of the radius. That precision holds for every shape you can dial
        up, all the way to <code class="code">superellipse(-3)</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
</header>
